Q: Track the scroll position of List sections? Nothing is working A: <p>As was established with your <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79858659/20386264">previous question</a>, the modifier <code>.scrollPosition</code> only works for a <code>ScrollView</code>, not for a <code>List</code>.</p>
<p>As a workaround, you can track the position of the rows yourself and update a state variable when a row is at a particular position, for example, at the top of the list.</p>
<ul>
<li>The modifier <code>.onGeometryChange</code> can be used to get the geometry of a row. This delivers a <code>GeometryProxy</code> which can be used to find the position (frame) of the row.</li>
<li>Unfortunately, <code>frame(in: .scrollView)</code> doesn't work for a <code>List</code> either, nor does a named coordinate space. So it is necessary to use the <code>.global</code> coordinate space.</li>
<li>This means, the position of the <code>List</code> in the global coordinate space is required too. This could be found in the same way, by applying an <code>.onGeometryChange</code> modifier to the <code>List</code> itself, but the result would need to be written to an extra state variable. Alternatively, you can just wrap the <code>List</code> with a <code>GeometryReader</code>. This way, an extra state variable is not needed.</li>
</ul>
<p>One way to implement the tracker is to encapsulate it as a <code>ViewModifier</code>. Here is a primitive implementation that works for a <code>String</code> id:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct PositionTracker: ViewModifier {
    let trackedId: String
    let yListTop: CGFloat
    @Binding var activeId: String?

    func body(content: Content) -&gt; some View {
        content
            .onGeometryChange(for: Bool.self) { geo in
                let frame = geo.frame(in: .global)
                return frame.minY - yListTop &lt;= frame.height &amp;&amp; frame.maxY - yListTop &gt; 0
            } action: { isTop in
                if isTop {
                    activeId = trackedId
                }
            }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>For convenience, a view extension can be defined:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>extension View {
    func trackPosition(trackedId: String, activeId: Binding&lt;String?&gt;, yListTop: CGFloat) -&gt; some View {
        modifier(PositionTracker(trackedId: trackedId, yListTop: yListTop, activeId: activeId))
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is how the tracker can be plugged into your example:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var activeSection: String?

    let sections = [
        &quot;2026-01-01&quot;,
        &quot;2026-01-02&quot;,
        &quot;2026-01-03&quot;,
        &quot;2026-01-04&quot;
    ]

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(&quot;Active section: \(activeSection ?? &quot;none&quot;)&quot;)
                .padding()

            GeometryReader { geo in // 👈 added
                let yListTop = geo.frame(in: .global).minY // 👈 added
                List {
                    ForEach(sections, id: \.self) { section in
                        Section {
                            ForEach(0..&lt;20, id: \.self) { index in
                                Text(&quot;Row \(index)&quot;)
                                    .id(&quot;\(section)-\(index)&quot;)
                                    .trackPosition( // 👈 added
                                        trackedId: &quot;\(section)-\(index)&quot;,
                                        activeId: $activeSection,
                                        yListTop: yListTop
                                    )
                            }
                        } header: {
                            Text(section)
                            // Attempt to observe section visibility
                                .id(section)
                        }
                    }
                }
                // .scrollPosition(id: $activeSection, anchor: .top) // 👈 removed
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/nCCncwPN.gif" alt="Screenshot" /></p>
<p>Some notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>There is no initial selection. This is also the way that <code>.scrollPostion</code> works.</li>
<li>If you want to be able to scroll to a row programmatically, the whole <code>List</code> could be wrapped with a <code>ScrollViewReader</code>. This does work for a <code>List</code>.</li>
<li>If you look closely, the selection does not change until the content of the row is actually touching the top of the <code>List</code>. This is because, the rows have some insets to increase their height to the minimum defined by the environment value <code>defaultMinListRowHeight</code>. You could refine the position detection logic by taking the minimum row height into consideration, if you wanted to.</li>
<li>Likewise, when scrolling between sections, the selection doesn't update until the first row of the new section is actually touching the top of the <code>List</code>. If you wanted to have a smooth continuation from one section to the next, you could consider suppyling a position tracker with the id of the <em>next</em> row. This could then be set when the (previous) row is detected to be just above the <code>List</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Btw, if the whole reason for using a <code>List</code> was because you want to have sections, don't forget that a <code>LazyVStack</code> can have sections too, see <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79324963/20386264">this answer</a> for an example. The modifier <code>.scrollPosition</code> would work for a <code>LazyVStack</code> inside a <code>ScrollView</code>.</p>
